Demo 2 is very informative. Parsing the diff: bars emit vertical rays toward the other bar at columns offset from the marker's position (bar_start + (marker_col − top_bar_start)); markers become 4 and emit a ray toward the nearest bar only (demo 1's marker was equidistant, so it shot both ways plus a horizontal connector; demo 2's markers were nearer the top bar, so only upward rays, stopping before markers/bar rows). Upward rays reach the top edge; downward rays stop before the bottom bar's row.

For the test: marker 6 at (18,9) is much nearer the bottom bar (dist 6 vs 18), so:
- top bar (start col 9) emits down ray at col 9+0=9: rows 1–17, stopping before the marker;
- marker shoots down: col 9, rows 19–23; marker cell becomes 4;
- bottom bar (start col 0) emits up ray at col 0+0=0: rows 0–23;
- no horizontal (marker not midway).

